# Exploring INEbase

```elixir
Mix.install([
  # To try a local checkout use {:inex, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")} instead
  {:inex, "~> 0.1"},
  {:kino_explorer, "~> 0.1"},
  {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"}
])
```

## Setup

INEbase is organised in **operations** (the CPI, the labour force survey...), which contain
**tables**, which contain **series**. Series are defined by the **values** of some **variables**
(province, sex, type of data...), and those values are what we use to filter tables and
operations. This notebook walks through that hierarchy.

```elixir
alias Explorer.DataFrame, as: DF
alias Explorer.Series, as: S
alias VegaLite, as: Vl

require Explorer.DataFrame

Inex.Telemetry.Logger.attach(level: :info)
```

## Operations

`Inex.Operations.list/1` returns every statistical operation available in the API.
`Inex.DataFrame.from_records/1` turns any metadata listing into a data frame, which Livebook
renders as an interactive table:

```elixir
{:ok, operations} = Inex.Operations.list()

operations = Inex.DataFrame.from_records(operations)
```

Let's look for the operations about prices. Since INE field names start with an uppercase letter,
we refer to them with `col/1` inside Explorer queries:

```elixir
DF.filter(operations, contains(col("Nombre"), "Precio"))
```

Operations can be referred to by their numeric id (`25`), their code (`"IPC"`) or their IOE code
(`"IOE30138"`):

```elixir
Inex.Operations.get("IOE30138")
```

## Tables

The tables of an operation are the ones you see in INEbase:

```elixir
{:ok, tables} = Inex.Operations.tables("IPC")

tables
|> Inex.DataFrame.from_records()
|> DF.select(["Id", "Nombre", "Codigo"])
```

Table `76136` holds the CPI by autonomous community (*comunidades autónomas*). Its URL in INEbase is
`https://www.ine.es/jaxiT3/Tabla.htm?t=76136`. `Inex.Tables.values/2` returns every variable and
value that defines it, which is all we need to filter it:

```elixir
{:ok, values} = Inex.Tables.values(76136)

values
|> Inex.DataFrame.from_records()
|> DF.select(["Variable.Id", "Variable.Nombre", "Id", "Nombre"])
```

## Filtering a Table

Filters are `{variable_id, value_id}` tuples. A list of values selects several of them and `:all`
selects every value of the variable. Let's get the annual change (`{3, 74}`) of the general index
(`{762, 304092}`) for every autonomous community (`{70, :all}`):

```elixir
regional_cpi =
  Inex.Tables.observations_as_data_frame!(76136,
    filters: [{3, 74}, {762, 304092}, {70, :all}],
    observation_start: ~D[2026-01-01]
  )
```

The data frame is **long** (one row per observation) and has one column per variable of the
table, so we can work with them directly. The most common variables have shortcuts borrowed from
the official `ineapir` R package, so the same query can be written as:

```elixir
Inex.Tables.observations_as_data_frame!(76136,
  filters: [data_type: 74, cpi_group: 304092, ccaa: :all],
  observation_start: ~D[2026-01-01]
)
|> DF.n_rows()
```

Now let's rank the regions by their latest annual inflation:

```elixir
latest_date = regional_cpi["date"] |> S.max()

latest =
  regional_cpi
  |> DF.filter(date == ^latest_date)
  |> DF.rename(%{"Comunidades y Ciudades Autónomas" => "region"})
  |> DF.select(["region", "value"])
  |> DF.sort_by(desc: value)

Vl.new(width: 600, height: 400, title: "CPI annual change by region - #{latest_date}")
|> Vl.data_from_values(latest)
|> Vl.mark(:bar)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "value", type: :quantitative, title: "%")
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "region", type: :nominal, sort: "-x", title: nil)
```

## Searching the Series of an Operation

You don't need to know the table: you can filter all the series of an operation by their
variables. First, the variables of the CPI:

```elixir
{:ok, variables} = Inex.Operations.variables("IPC")

Inex.DataFrame.from_records(variables)
```

And the values of one of them, the provinces (`115`):

```elixir
{:ok, provinces} = Inex.Operations.variable_values("IPC", 115)

provinces
|> Inex.DataFrame.from_records()
|> DF.select(["Id", "Nombre", "Codigo"])
```

Note the difference between the `Id` of a value, used in filters, and its official `Codigo`: the
province of Madrid has the `Id` `29` and the code `"28"` (the one used in postal codes).

`Inex.Series.search/2` returns the series matching the filters. Here: the monthly series of the
province of Madrid (`29`) with the annual change (`74`) of every ECOICOP group:

```elixir
{:ok, madrid_series} =
  Inex.Series.search("IPC",
    filters: [province: 29, data_type: 74, cpi_group: :all],
    periodicity: :monthly
  )

madrid_series
|> Inex.DataFrame.from_records()
|> DF.select(["COD", "Nombre"])
```

`Inex.Series.search_observations_as_data_frame/2` returns their data directly. Let's compare the
inflation of every province:

```elixir
by_province =
  Inex.Series.search_observations_as_data_frame!("IPC",
    filters: [data_type: 74, cpi_group: 304092, province: :all],
    periodicity: :monthly,
    observation_start: ~D[2025-01-01]
  )
  |> DF.rename(%{"Provincias" => "province"})

Vl.new(width: 700, height: 350, title: "CPI annual change by province (%)")
|> Vl.data_from_values(DF.select(by_province, ["date", "province", "value"]))
|> Vl.mark(:boxplot)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "date", type: :ordinal, time_unit: :yearmonth, title: nil)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "value", type: :quantitative, title: "%")
```

## PC-Axis and tpx Tables

Some tables are not stored in Tempus3 but in PC-Axis or tpx files. Their ids come from the INEbase
URL: `https://www.ine.es/jaxi/Tabla.htm?path=/t20/e245/p08/l0/&file=01001.px` becomes
`"t20/e245/p08/l0/01001.px"`. They are filtered by codes instead of numeric ids, and
`Inex.Tables.values/2` gives us those codes too:

```elixir
px_table = "t20/e245/p08/l0/01001.px"

{:ok, px_values} = Inex.Tables.values(px_table)

Inex.DataFrame.from_records(px_values)
```

```elixir
Inex.Tables.observations_as_data_frame!(px_table,
  filters: [
    {"sexo", :all},
    {"espanolesextranjeros", ["espanoles", "extranjeros"]},
    {"edad3gruposdeedad", "totaledades"}
  ]
)
|> DF.select(["name", "period", "value", "Sexo", "Españoles/Extranjeros"])
```

## Hierarchies

Values are organised in hierarchies. `Inex.Variables.children/3` returns the children of a value,
for example the provinces of Galicia (variable `70`, value `9008`):

```elixir
{:ok, galicia} = Inex.Variables.children(70, 9008)

galicia
|> Inex.DataFrame.from_records()
|> DF.select(["Id", "Nombre", "Codigo"])
```

## Release Calendar

Publications hold the release dates of every operation, past and scheduled:

```elixir
{:ok, publications} = Inex.Operations.publications("IPC")

Inex.DataFrame.from_records(publications)
```

```elixir
{:ok, dates} = Inex.Publications.dates(8, friendly: true)

today = Date.utc_today()

dates
|> Enum.map(&%{name: &1["Nombre"], date: &1["Fecha"] |> String.slice(0, 10) |> Date.from_iso8601!()})
|> Enum.filter(&(Date.compare(&1.date, today) != :lt))
|> Enum.take(6)
|> DF.new()
```

The *Spain in Charts* notebook puts all of this to work: it draws the charts of the
[Inex demo](https://jaimeiniesta.com/en/inex) with the same calls.
